{"name":"Canadiana","title":"Canadiana","revision":236505,"url":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Canadiana","retrieved":"2026-09-06","license":"CC BY-SA","licenseUrl":"https://www.fandom.com/licensing","language":"en","blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","text":"Canadiana refers to both media and iconography distinctive of Canada."},{"kind":"paragraph","text":"It can also refer to an aesthetic lifestyle, and a distinct interior design that features warm, cozy comfort with rugged wilderness (such as the autumn season) roots. It heavily drew inspiration from Canada's vast natural, historic trade eras, harsh winters, and seasonal landscapes where it balances rustic, raw elements such as heavy wools and exposed timbers with a welcoming, clean simplicity as a whole vibe of a country."},{"kind":"paragraph","text":"it is equivelent to Americana and Australiana."},{"kind":"heading","text":"History"},{"kind":"paragraph","text":"The aesthetic was solidified in the 1960s."},{"kind":"heading","text":"Visuals"},{"kind":"paragraph","text":"In home decor, this architectural style replicates a stone fireplace, modern northern wilderness lodge, nostalgic vintage accents and layering natural wood tones to build a warm sanctuary against the cold (such as the winter season)."},{"kind":"paragraph","text":"More regional varients are:"},{"kind":"list","text":"Prairie"},{"kind":"list","text":"Maritimes"},{"kind":"list","text":"Montreal"},{"kind":"paragraph","text":"Materials featured include:"},{"kind":"list","text":"Wood"},{"kind":"list","text":"Taxidermy animals and hides"},{"kind":"heading","text":"Media"},{"kind":"heading","text":"Art"},{"kind":"list","text":"Emily Carr"},{"kind":"list","text":"Alex Colville"},{"kind":"list","text":"Cornelius Krieghoff"},{"kind":"list","text":"Maud Lewis"},{"kind":"heading","text":"Movies"},{"kind":"heading","text":"Television"},{"kind":"list","text":"National Film Board Log Driver's Waltz (1979)"},{"kind":"heading","text":"Toys"},{"kind":"list","text":"Maplelina"},{"kind":"heading","text":"Music"},{"kind":"list","text":"Great Blue Sea"},{"kind":"list","text":"The Tragically Hip"},{"kind":"list","text":"Stompin' Tom Connors"},{"kind":"list","text":"Tom Cochrane"},{"kind":"heading","text":"Misc"},{"kind":"list","text":"Frontier Canada at Canada's Wonderland"},{"kind":"list","text":"Little Canada"}],"contentStatus":"full-source","images":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/e/ef/0014e876cb01c960d164efaa9739afe1.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260904024607","caption":"0014e876cb01c960d164efaa9739afe1","source":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/File:0014e876cb01c960d164efaa9739afe1.jpg","remote":true,"local":"images/detail/0161-1.webp","localAvailable":true},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/4/45/Sugar_Making_in_Canada%2C_1852._By_Cornelius_Krieghoff_%281815-1872%29.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260904142751","caption":"Canadiana · Visual reference 02","source":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/File:Sugar_Making_in_Canada%2C_1852._By_Cornelius_Krieghoff_%281815-1872%29.jpg","remote":true,"local":"images/detail/0161-2.webp","localAvailable":true}]}